#
# verify-padding — assert the synthetic fleet's objects are the size real
# ones are. Spike S8's durable half.
#
# The measurement in internal/watch/objectsize_test.go establishes what a
# Pod and a Node actually weigh; padding the templates to match is a
# one-time act; this is what stops them drifting back. Without it the
# failure is silent and slow: somebody trims a template to make a
# scenario readable, every scale number in §6.6 quietly gets better, and
# nothing anywhere says the fixture stopped resembling a cluster.
#
# It measures the APPLIED objects rather than the manifests, because the
# largest single thing a manifest cannot carry is managedFields — the
# apiserver writes that itself, and it is real bulk.
#
# Run after scale-up; scale-up runs it for you.
#
#   examples/kwok/verify-padding

set -euo pipefail
. "$(dirname "$0")/lib.sh"

require_kwok_installed

if ! ((KWOK_PAD)); then
  echo "▸ padding check skipped: KWOK_PAD=0"
  echo "  This run's object sizes are not comparable to §6.6 — do not quote"
  echo "  its numbers as a scale result."
  exit 0
fi

# mean_size <kubectl-args...> — mean serialised bytes of the listed
# objects.
#
# --show-managed-fields is not optional and is the whole reason this
# function exists rather than being inlined. kubectl STRIPS managedFields
# from -o json by default, and managedFields is 5–6 KB of a pod: without
# the flag this reads a third under the truth, and a correctly padded
# fixture fails its own check.
#
# tojson is the compact form, which is what the Go measurement in
# internal/watch/objectsize_test.go produces too. The two agreed to within
# 31 bytes on the same cluster, which is the only reason the targets below
# can be quoted from one and asserted by the other.
mean_size() {
  kubectl "$@" --show-managed-fields -o json |
    jq -r '[.items[] | (. | tojson | length)] | if length == 0 then "0 0" else "\(add) \(length)" end' |
    awk '{ if ($2 == 0) print 0; else printf "%d\n", $1 / $2 }'
}

# check <label> <measured> <floor> <target>
#
# The floor is enforced; the target is reported. A fixture below the floor
# has been trimmed and the run is worthless. A fixture between the two is
# the expected state — see lib.sh for the two shortfalls that are
# structural — and printing the ratio is how that stays visible rather
# than becoming folklore.
check() {
  local label="$1" got="$2" floor="$3" target="$4"
  if ((got < floor)); then
    echo "  ✗ $label: mean ${got} B, below the ${floor} B floor" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  echo "  ✓ $label: mean ${got} B — ${floor} B floor, $((got * 100 / target))% of the ${target} B real-cluster p50"
}

echo "▸ checking the synthetic fleet is the weight of a real one"

pods="$(mean_size get pods -A -l "$FLEET_LABEL")"
nodes="$(mean_size get nodes -l "$FAKE_NODE_LABEL")"

if ((pods == 0)); then
  echo "ERROR: no fleet pods found — run examples/kwok/scale-up first" >&2
  exit 1
fi

rc=0
check "Pod " "$pods" "$PAD_POD_FLOOR_BYTES" "$PAD_POD_TARGET_BYTES" || rc=1
check "Node" "$nodes" "$PAD_NODE_FLOOR_BYTES" "$PAD_NODE_TARGET_BYTES" || rc=1

if ((rc)); then
  cat >&2 <<EOF

The fixture has deflated. kwok objects that are lighter than real ones
make every §6.6 budget pass while validating nothing, and understate the
CPU model by the same factor, since decode cost scales with wire size.

Either restore the padding (pad_node_images / pad_pod_annotations /
pad_pod_env in examples/kwok/lib.sh), or — if real objects have genuinely
got smaller — re-measure and move the targets:

  LOOKOUT_MEASURE_CONTEXT=<ctx> go test ./internal/watch -run ObjectSizes -v
EOF
  exit 1
fi
